# Condition B, round 2

## Observed evidence and history
- B_01 increased training horizon from 30 to 300 and was kept: joint validation objective 0.854255 (baseline 1.0).
- Forecast NRMSE improved from 0.805071 to 0.449039. Mean errors at 300/900 steps fell from 0.667546/1.637757 to 0.375743/0.683669.
- The expected control benefit was not observed: cost increased from 1.845437 to 2.123631. Mean 30-step forecast error increased from 0.109911 to 0.287705, worsening every output. Oxygen tracking errors rose from 0.364633/0.365829 to 0.434016/0.691831. This suggests a short/long horizon tradeoff, but does not establish that forecast errors caused the control regression.
- B_01 used 34 episodes and 70,519 optimizer updates. No proposal has been discarded yet.

## Single round-2 proposal
Retain 300-step training rollouts and replace uniform rollout MSE with 0.5 * mean MSE over steps 1–30 + 0.5 * mean MSE over steps 1–300, in fixed normalized output coordinates. This raises the first 30 steps from 10% to 55% of total temporal weight without another rollout. Architecture, optimizer, history, ensemble and pipeline remain as accepted in B_01.

## Explicit condition B sampling choice
Retain full [0,1] fractional bounds and slow_probability 0.5 for all four actions. Actual training coverage spans 98.3–100% of each physical envelope, occupies all ten deciles for every action, and has positive input covariance eigenvalues (0.0645–0.0981). Validation bins contain at least 2400 observations; residual error is highest at middle stoker (0.523), low feed (0.563), high primary (0.531), and high secondary (0.516), with low secondary nearly equal (0.509). These marginal diagnostics provide no clear isolated region to target. Preserve broad spatial coverage and both fast and sustained excitation while testing the loss tradeoff.

## Expected effect, uncertainty and next checks
Expect lower 30-step NRMSE and lower control cost relative to B_01 while preserving enough 300/900-step accuracy to improve the joint objective. Inspect oxygen tracking and scenario overshoot as well as all-channel horizon errors. Long-horizon accuracy may regress; short-horizon fit may not resolve incorrect action sensitivities or linear-model limitations. Additional broad episodes and timed-update variability limit causal attribution. The runner must measure both forecasting and control; no round-2 outcome is available. No training, simulation or evaluation was run in this turn.
